course: dictionary
lesson: 9275
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: A word can have more than one meaning, and the sentence tells you which one. «Start digging in a garden and a robin will often hop down beside your spade» is about digging, so spade here is a garden tool.
explain: «In playing cards the Knave is the old name for the Jack, and in the book the four suits have jobs: spades are gardeners.» The clue is playing cards, so spades here is a suit, one of the four groups of cards.
explain: Check by swapping in a meaning. Does «a garden tool» still make sense in the sentence? Then that is the right meaning. Does «a suit of cards» fit the sentence about digging? No, so you know which meaning to use.
fact: A spade is a garden tool with a long handle and a flat metal blade, used for digging.
fact: A spade can also be a small plastic tool for digging in sand at the beach, as in «bucket and spade».
fact: Spades are one of the four suits in a pack of playing cards, shown by a black ♠ shape.
fact: «Call a spade a spade» is a phrase that means say things plainly and honestly.
exercise: «Start digging in a garden and a robin will often hop down beside your spade within minutes.» What is a spade in this sentence? | A suit of playing cards | *A tool for digging in a garden | A small boat | A kind of bird
exercise: «In the book, spades are gardeners.» Which suit is the gardener suit? | hearts | clubs | diamonds | *spades
exercise: The phrase «call a spade a spade» means what? | Play a card game with friends | Hit something with a tool | Dig a hole in the garden | *Say things plainly and honestly
test: Which meaning fits a «bucket and spade» at the beach? | A heart on a playing card | *A small plastic tool for digging in sand | A long wooden ladder
test: «Pigs dig up roots and grubs with their snout, like a living spade.» What does the spade comparison suggest? | Singing a song | *Digging up things from the ground | Swimming in a pond | Carrying food in a bucket
exam: Jayden says a spade can only ever be a garden tool. Is he right? | *No, it can also mean a playing card suit or a beach tool | Yes, because the word has only one meaning | No, but it can only mean a bird | Yes, it can only ever mean a garden tool
